## The binder organizes; item type decides structure

The binder tree on the side is for arranging things the way that helps you work. It does not decide what your book looks like once compiled: that job belongs to each item's *type*. Skribisto reads a project as one flat, ordered list of typed rows, and the type of each row is what a Book, Part, or Chapter actually *is*. Nest freely; nesting is for you, not what shapes the book.

Every writing item, whatever its type, carries a main text and a synopsis side by side, so you plan in one pane while drafting in the other.

## The types you can create

- *Book*: as simple or layered as you like, from a few scenes to nested parts and chapters.
- *Part*: gathers chapters under one heading, running until the next part or the book's end.
- *Chapter*: runs until the next chapter, part, or the book's end. Two shapes, below.
- *Scene*: your writing, with its own synopsis.
- *Note*: text and a synopsis, for research, asides, or reminders. It can still reach the compiled book if you choose to include notes.
- *Note folder*: a note that also holds other notes beneath it, for grouping them.
- *Folder*: a plain container. It carries a synopsis but no prose of its own; the items inside hold the writing.
- *Paratext*, *Paratext folder*, *End of Book*: below.

## Two shapes of chapter

A chapter has two shapes that read identically in the finished book. A chapter folder holds scenes underneath it in the binder. A flat chapter is a single row you write straight into, with no scenes below it. Both take prose directly; the only difference is whether the chapter can also contain scenes.

Which shape a *new* chapter takes is set in Settings, under Work, then Structure: the *Flat chapters* toggle. On means single rows; off means folders that can also hold scenes. Existing chapters keep their shape regardless.

To change one chapter's shape, or any item's type, right-click it (or open its Inspector) and choose *Convert to*. Converting a chapter folder to a flat chapter, or back, keeps its title and prose; only an empty one can become flat, since a flat chapter has nowhere to put child items. *Convert to* reaches further: a plain folder can become a chapter folder, part, book, or note folder and back; a scene can become a note or a flat chapter, either converting back to a scene. Whatever a conversion cannot carry over, Skribisto names plainly and refuses rather than discarding it quietly: a chapter with its own prose cannot become a Part, which has nowhere to keep scene text.

## Why a book needs an End of Book marker

Skribisto keeps every book in one continuous list, so a book's end is not decided by nesting: one project can hold more than one book, end to end. The *End of Book* row marks where one stops; anything after it belongs to the next.

## Paratext: part of the book, not the story

A *Paratext* is writing that belongs to the book but not to its story: a preface, dedication, afterword, or colophon. It exports wherever you place it, but is never counted in the word count, since conventions for where it goes differ by country and publisher. A *Paratext folder* is purely organizational: it keeps paratexts out of the rest of the binder but contributes nothing to the exported book, not even its own name.

New to Skribisto? [Getting started](:help-getting-started) covers opening and saving a project first.
